13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1a",null,{"guide":{"date":"July 5–18, 1864","place":"Georgia, Alabama, Virginia, Maryland, and Washington, D.C.","result":"Sherman began moving around Atlanta while events in Virginia and the 1864 election increased the political importance of the campaign","facts":[{"label":"Atlanta objective","value":"Cut the Georgia Railroad east of the city"},{"label":"Rousseau raid","value":"About 2,500 U.S. cavalrymen","note":"They destroyed roughly 30 miles of railroad in Alabama."},{"label":"Sherman’s advance","value":"Thomas on the west, Schofield in the center, McPherson on the east"},{"label":"Virginia campaign losses described","value":"About 65,000 U.S. casualties during the Overland Campaign"},{"label":"Political setting","value":"The presidential election was scheduled for November 1864"}],"maps":[{"title":"The Atlanta campaign from Rome to Marietta","caption":"Blue lines show U.S. movements and red lines show Confederate movements. The map places the Chattahoochee crossings and the advance toward Atlanta within the wider campaign.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008628278/","image":"/images/atlanta-campaign-map-3.jpg","alt":"U.S. Army map of the Atlanta campaign from Rome and Kingston south to Dallas and Marietta"},{"title":"From Kennesaw Mountain to Atlanta","caption":"This campaign map shows Marietta, the Chattahoochee River, Roswell, Peachtree Creek, Decatur, Atlanta, and the military works and movements between them.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008628278/","image":"/images/atlanta-campaign-map-4.jpg","alt":"U.S. Army map of operations from Kennesaw Mountain to Atlanta in 1864"},{"title":"Atlanta and its eastern railroad today","caption":"Sherman planned to move from the Chattahoochee crossings around the north and east of Atlanta and cut the Georgia Railroad between Stone Mountain and Decatur.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-84.67%2C33.55%2C-83.96%2C34.09&layer=mapnik&marker=33.755%2C-84.39"},{"title":"Petersburg, Washington, and the Shenandoah Valley","caption":"Grant remained outside Petersburg while Jubal Early moved down the Shenandoah Valley, crossed the Potomac, defeated a U.S. force at Monocacy, and reached the defenses of Washington.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-79.70%2C36.70%2C-75.40%2C40.20&layer=mapnik&marker=38.9072%2C-77.0369"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:17","audioTime":77,"title":"The episode returns to the Chattahoochee mills","detail":"When Johnston abandoned the Shoup Line, U.S. troops gained an industrial district along the river. Cavalrymen removed workers and burned mills that produced cloth for Confederate uniforms."},{"time":"2:49","audioTime":169,"title":"Johnston formed a line south of Peachtree Creek","detail":"The Army of Tennessee occupied ground about three miles north of central Atlanta. Wheeler covered the front and William H. Jackson held the Confederate left."},{"time":"3:48","audioTime":228,"title":"Alexander P. Stewart took command of Polk’s former corps","detail":"Stewart assumed corps command on July 7. William W. Loring returned to his division, and Jefferson Davis selected Henry D. Clayton to replace Stewart as a division commander."},{"time":"6:00","audioTime":360,"title":"Sherman planned to isolate Atlanta by rail","detail":"Sherman intended to avoid a direct attack on the city defenses and make Atlanta unusable by cutting the railroads that connected it to the rest of the Confederacy."},{"time":"7:37","audioTime":457,"title":"Two cavalry raids targeted railroads west of Atlanta","detail":"Lovell Rousseau led a long-range raid from Tennessee into Alabama while George Stoneman crossed the Chattahoochee southwest of Atlanta."},{"time":"8:08","audioTime":488,"title":"Rousseau damaged the railroad in Alabama","detail":"His 2,500 cavalrymen destroyed supplies, bridges, rolling stock, and about 30 miles of track. The damage interrupted Confederate communications for weeks."},{"time":"9:21","audioTime":561,"title":"Stoneman withdrew after crossing the river","detail":"Stoneman captured a damaged bridge, crossed on July 14, then retreated when Confederate troops opposed him. Sherman ended that part of the operation."},{"time":"10:06","audioTime":606,"title":"Sherman began the movement around Atlanta","detail":"McPherson moved from Roswell toward the Georgia Railroad. Schofield advanced in the center and Thomas served as the western hinge of the movement."},{"time":"11:14","audioTime":674,"title":"U.S. armies advanced to Nancy Creek","detail":"On July 17, the three armies pushed back Wheeler’s cavalry and moved about halfway from the Chattahoochee to Peachtree Creek."},{"time":"12:05","audioTime":725,"title":"The episode shifts to the wider war","detail":"Jubal Early crossed the Potomac on July 5, defeated a U.S. force at Monocacy on July 9, and reached the outskirts of Washington two days later."},{"time":"14:17","audioTime":857,"title":"Grant remained outside Richmond and Petersburg","detail":"After the Overland Campaign and the first attacks on Petersburg, the Army of the Potomac was exhausted and unable to force an immediate breakthrough."},{"time":"19:40","audioTime":1180,"title":"The 1864 election affected military strategy","detail":"Republican divisions, Democratic opposition to the war and emancipation, and rising war weariness made a clear U.S. military success politically important."},{"time":"28:06","audioTime":1686,"title":"Atlanta became a major political objective","detail":"With Grant stopped outside Petersburg and Early threatening Washington, the outcome of Sherman’s campaign carried greater importance for both governments."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Abraham Lincoln","command":"President of the United States","subordinates":"Led the administration during the 1864 election campaign"},{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-Chief","subordinates":"Directed U.S. operations from the Petersburg front"},{"commander":"William T. Sherman","command":"Military Division of the Mississippi","subordinates":"George H. Thomas, John M. Schofield, and James B. McPherson"},{"commander":"Lovell Rousseau","command":"Alabama cavalry raid","subordinates":"About 2,500 mounted troops"},{"commander":"George Stoneman","command":"Army of the Ohio cavalry division","subordinates":"Operated southwest of Atlanta"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Jefferson Davis","command":"President of the Confederate States","subordinates":"Approved senior appointments in the Army of Tennessee"},{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Held Richmond and Petersburg and detached Early’s force"},{"commander":"Jubal A. Early","command":"Valley force","subordinates":"Moved through the Shenandoah Valley into Maryland and toward Washington"},{"commander":"Joseph E. Johnston","command":"Army of Tennessee","subordinates":"John B. Hood, William J. Hardee, and Alexander P. Stewart"},{"commander":"Joseph Wheeler","command":"Cavalry Corps","subordinates":"Covered the Confederate front north of Atlanta"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Lovell Rousseau","role":"Led the successful U.S. cavalry raid against railroads and supplies in Alabama","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Stoneman","role":"Led the unsuccessful cavalry operation southwest of Atlanta","side":"United States"},{"name":"Alexander P. Stewart","role":"Assumed command of Leonidas Polk’s former corps on July 7","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jubal A. Early","role":"Led the Confederate campaign into Maryland and to the defenses of Washington","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"Sought reelection while military setbacks increased political opposition","side":"United States"},{"name":"John C. Frémont","role":"Radical Republican presidential candidate who opposed Lincoln","side":"United States"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Leading candidate for the Democratic presidential nomination","side":"United States"},{"name":"Clement L. Vallandigham","role":"Prominent Peace Democrat who opposed continuation of the war","side":"United States"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/atlanta-railroad-depot.jpg","alt":"Atlanta railroad depot photographed in 1864","caption":"Atlanta’s railroad network was the principal military target of Sherman’s movement around the city.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666982/"},{"image":"/images/atlanta-fortifications.jpg","alt":"Confederate earthwork fortifications at Atlanta in 1864","caption":"Sherman planned to bypass Atlanta’s fortifications and cut the railroads instead of attacking the defenses directly.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670937/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Episode 533 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2026/07/12/civil-war-podcast-episode-533/"},{"label":"Local Episode 533 transcript","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2026/07/12/civil-war-podcast-episode-533/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History campaign study","url":"https://history.army.mil/Publications/Publications-Catalog/The-Atlanta-and-Savannah-Campaigns/"},{"label":"Library of Congress Atlanta campaign map series","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008628278/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Monocacy","url":"https://www.nps.gov/mono/learn/historyculture/the-battle-of-monocacy.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Senate: The Election of 1864","url":"https://www.senate.gov/artandhistory/history/minute/The_Election_of_1864.htm"}]}}]}]
